<p>The two-year period must be continuous and must immediately precede the presentation of the petition. Both the factum and the animus must co-exist through it — separation that begins innocently (a posting abroad, a medical stay) becomes desertion only from the point the intention to abandon the marriage forms, and the clock runs from then.</p>

<h2>Where desertion petitions fail</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The expelled spouse.</strong> The commonest failure: the petitioner&rsquo;s own conduct caused the exit. Constructive desertion reverses the roles, and the bars in Section 23(1)(a) close the door on relief founded on one&rsquo;s own wrong.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The premature petition.</strong> Filing before two complete years of qualifying desertion — including where an intervening resumption of cohabitation restarted the period — is fatal, because the period is a condition of the cause of action.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The silent record.</strong> Petitions that prove absence but not intention. Years apart, without evidence that the respondent repudiated the marriage — no refusal of reconciliation, no severed communication, nothing — leave the animus unestablished.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The ignored overture.</strong> A respondent&rsquo;s genuine attempts to return, rebuffed by the petitioner, can convert the petitioner into the party preventing cohabitation.</div>
</div>

<h2>Wilful neglect: the overlooked limb</h2>
<p>The statutory Explanation includes wilful neglect within desertion. This reaches the spouse who, without physically leaving, deliberately and persistently fails in the elementary obligations of marriage — maintenance, companionship, consortium — in a manner showing the marriage has been abandoned in substance. It is a demanding standard: neglect must be wilful, not the product of incapacity, illness or estrangement short of repudiation. But it matters in households where a spouse has withdrawn into total indifference while declining to depart, leaving the other formally married and functionally alone.</p>

<h2>Building (and defending) the case</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Petitioners: chronicle, don&rsquo;t characterise.</strong> Dates of departure, attempts at reconciliation with particulars, letters and messages seeking return, panchayat or family interventions — the animus is proved by the paper trail of rebuffed effort.</p>
  <p><strong>Plead around Section 23.</strong> Anticipate the constructive-desertion answer: explain the circumstances of the separation and the petitioner&rsquo;s clean hands expressly.</p>
  <p><strong>Respondents: establish cause or consent.</strong> Evidence of cruelty, of expulsion, of an agreed separation, or of genuine offers to return meets the ground at its weakest element — and a maintenance petition or DV complaint filed during the period often documents the cause contemporaneously.</p>
  <p><strong>Both: watch the two-year arithmetic.</strong> Identify the date animus formed, any interruptions, and the filing date. Desertion cases are won and lost on the calendar as often as on the conduct.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer states general principles under the Hindu Marriage Act and is not advice on any particular matter. Parallel grounds exist with variations under other personal laws and the Special Marriage Act, 1954.</div>
